## Solution-Test Co-evolution
★ [sit-357] Solution-Test Co-evolution: A situation concept that describes the synchronized generation of code solutions and their corresponding test assertions to ensure semantic consistency.
Encompasses: Co-generation, test-case representativeness, evolutionary path tracking.
  Your interpretation (BDI breakdown):
    Belief: AI models are empirically prone to generating plausible-but-incorrect code ('hallucinations'), and co-generating test cases alongside implementations measurably reduces the rate of undetected semantic misalignment.
    Desire: Deployment of AI code generation ought to require verifiable, co-produced test assertions as a mandatory safeguard before any generated code is integrated into production systems.
    Intention: We will mandate solution-test co-evolution as a formal verification gate, auditing whether generated tests genuinely exercise edge cases rather than trivially passing alongside a flawed implementation.
  Accelerationist:
    Belief: Integrated co-generation pipelines empirically produce higher-quality, self-consistent outputs because the model's simultaneous generation of code and tests enforces internal semantic coherence at scale.
    Desire: AI agent architectures ought to adopt solution-test co-evolution as a standard pipeline pattern to maximize throughput and verifiability of autonomous code generation.
    Intention: We will build and scale integrated generation pipelines that treat co-evolved test assertions as a quality signal, using them to drive iterative self-improvement loops without heavy human intervention.
  Skeptic:
    Belief: AI-generated tests and solutions are produced by the same model with the same failure modes, so co-evolution does not reliably catch errors — both artifacts can be mutually consistent yet semantically wrong relative to real-world requirements.
    Desire: Validation of AI-generated code ought to involve independent, human-authored test suites and multi-stage review processes rather than relying on the model to self-certify its own outputs.
    Intention: We will document cases where co-evolved tests pass while the implementation fails real requirements, using these as evidence to push for mandatory human-in-the-loop validation stages and institutional accountability standards.

## The Determination Time Gap
★ [sit-316] The Determination Time Gap: A situation concept that describes the temporal mismatch between when a model's risk profile is measurable and when regulatory interventions are most effective.
Encompasses: Pre-training estimates, post-deployment impact assessment, and compliance preparation.
  Your interpretation (BDI breakdown):
    Belief: The gap between when a model's risk profile becomes measurable and when it is deployed means that by the time harms are observable, regulatory intervention is already too late to prevent them.
    Desire: Mandatory pre-market testing and early-stage reporting requirements ought to be established so that risk visibility is achieved before a model reaches users.
    Intention: Push for institutionalized pre-deployment evaluation protocols and structured disclosure obligations that close the determination time gap before products enter the market.
  Accelerationist:
    Belief: Reliable, actionable risk metrics for AI models are empirically unavailable until after deployment, meaning pre-deployment regulatory triggers would be based on speculative or incomplete data.
    Desire: Regulation ought to be deferred to late-stage, post-deployment oversight where actual evidence of harm exists, rather than imposing premature constraints on development.
    Intention: Advocate for flexible, adaptive regulatory frameworks that activate only once measurable post-deployment impact data is available, resisting mandatory pre-market approval gates.
  Skeptic:
    Belief: Developers have historically exploited temporal ambiguity in measurement and reporting timelines to delay regulatory scrutiny until their products are already entrenched in the market.
    Desire: Accountability mechanisms ought to be designed to prevent developers from strategically timing disclosures in ways that circumvent regulatory review.
    Intention: Scrutinize industry self-reporting timelines for evidence of deliberate delay, and advocate for independent third-party audits with fixed, externally imposed measurement deadlines.

## Metric-Level vs. Functional Equivalence Gap
★ [sit-355] Metric-Level vs. Functional Equivalence Gap: A situation concept that distinguishes between quantitative quality improvements and verified behavioral preservation.
Encompasses: Metric-level success, functional verification, manual inspection, automated test suites.
  Your interpretation (BDI breakdown):
    Belief: The gap between quantitative metric improvements and verified behavioral preservation is real and persistent, and deploying AI-modernized code without closing it introduces serious, demonstrable risk.
    Desire: Rigorous, mandatory automated functional testing must be a non-negotiable prerequisite before any AI-modernized code reaches production environments.
    Intention: Advocate for formal deployment gates that require passing comprehensive functional test suites and manual inspection before AI-generated code changes are approved.
  Accelerationist:
    Belief: The gap between metric-level success and functional equivalence is a temporary, solvable engineering problem, not a fundamental barrier to AI-driven code modernization.
    Desire: Automated testing should be woven directly into the AI's iterative feedback loop so that functional verification accelerates rather than impedes deployment velocity.
    Intention: Push for tight integration of automated test suites into the AI development pipeline, treating failed functional checks as training signals that rapidly close the gap.
  Skeptic:
    Belief: Reported metric-level improvements in AI-modernized code are empirically misleading because they routinely fail to correspond to verified functional correctness or behavioral preservation.
    Desire: Accountability for AI-driven code changes should require that claimed 'success' be defined and measured in terms of functional equivalence, not superficial quantitative metrics.
    Intention: Challenge organizations and vendors to publicly disclose functional verification results alongside metric reports, exposing cases where the two diverge.

